WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.100 --> 00:00:03.840
- All right, it looks like
I'm online on Facebook.

00:00:03.840 --> 00:00:05.470
Hello, everyone at Facebook.

00:00:05.470 --> 00:00:07.440
Sorry, running a little bit late.

00:00:07.440 --> 00:00:10.590
If you ask, I'll tell you
about my morning (chuckles).

00:00:10.590 --> 00:00:13.640
And it looks like we're
online on YouTube, all right.

00:00:13.640 --> 00:00:15.140
So this is good.

00:00:15.140 --> 00:00:17.700
As you can imagine, this
has been an interesting

00:00:17.700 --> 00:00:20.533
improvisational situation for all of us,

00:00:21.420 --> 00:00:23.330
so my apologies for getting started

00:00:23.330 --> 00:00:25.230
a little late this morning.

00:00:25.230 --> 00:00:26.500
But this is what,

00:00:26.500 --> 00:00:28.310
our fourth live stream that we're doing?

00:00:28.310 --> 00:00:31.050
And, you know, the whole goal of this is

00:00:31.050 --> 00:00:33.150
we have all of these
resources at Khan Academy

00:00:33.150 --> 00:00:34.838
that we've been building over the last 10,

00:00:34.838 --> 00:00:37.240
actually, 11, 12 years.

00:00:37.240 --> 00:00:40.447
And we could've never foreseen
this type of a situation.

00:00:40.447 --> 00:00:43.440
You know, Khan Academy,
we've always imagined

00:00:43.440 --> 00:00:47.080
the ideal use case being
used in physical classrooms,

00:00:47.080 --> 00:00:48.700
so students could learn at
their own time and pace,

00:00:48.700 --> 00:00:51.510
give teachers information
on where students are,

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:53.300
and be able to do more
focused interventions

00:00:53.300 --> 00:00:54.790
for your classtime for other things.

00:00:54.790 --> 00:00:56.496
And also support students
who might not have had

00:00:56.496 --> 00:00:58.620
traditional schooling or
if you're in some part

00:00:58.620 --> 00:01:00.230
of the world that doesn't have that.

00:01:00.230 --> 00:01:01.685
We would've never foreseen this

00:01:01.685 --> 00:01:04.400
school closure situation we're in.

00:01:04.400 --> 00:01:08.040
But Khan Academy is eerily well-suited

00:01:08.040 --> 00:01:11.020
to at least help us work on this.

00:01:11.020 --> 00:01:13.580
Because obviously, we have
things from Khan Academy Kids,

00:01:13.580 --> 00:01:15.940
for early learners, for math, reading,

00:01:15.940 --> 00:01:17.440
writing, social-emotional learning.

00:01:17.440 --> 00:01:18.985
And then you get into
the core of Khan Academy,

00:01:18.985 --> 00:01:22.000
in math for sure, all the way through

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:23.370
from middle school, high school,

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:25.030
elementary school through college,

00:01:25.030 --> 00:01:28.350
English language arts, SAT Practice.

00:01:28.350 --> 00:01:30.590
We have the sciences, especially
at the high school level,

00:01:30.590 --> 00:01:33.150
AP level, and also several humanities,

00:01:33.150 --> 00:01:34.790
American history, et cetera.

00:01:34.790 --> 00:01:37.060
And the purpose of this live stream

00:01:37.060 --> 00:01:40.140
is to give a little bit
more connectedness to this.

00:01:40.140 --> 00:01:40.973
Some people are starting

00:01:40.973 --> 00:01:42.810
to call this kind of a
homeroom for us (chuckles).

00:01:42.810 --> 00:01:44.610
And like homeroom is in school,

00:01:44.610 --> 00:01:45.960
it's the time to talk about anything,

00:01:45.960 --> 00:01:49.150
a time, whatever announcements
need to be made, et cetera,

00:01:49.150 --> 00:01:50.800
and also just get a
little bit of motivation,

00:01:50.800 --> 00:01:52.070
a little bit of energy for the day.

00:01:52.070 --> 00:01:53.933
Whether you're a teacher,
you're a parent or a student,

00:01:53.933 --> 00:01:56.040
this is just a reminder

00:01:56.040 --> 00:01:58.325
that we're all going
through this together,

00:01:58.325 --> 00:02:01.270
and we're all gonna
figure it out together.

00:02:01.270 --> 00:02:03.460
And so I encourage anyone,

00:02:03.460 --> 00:02:05.630
start asking questions on,

00:02:05.630 --> 00:02:07.640
whether you're watching
on Facebook or YouTube.

00:02:07.640 --> 00:02:09.820
On the streams, I have team members

00:02:09.820 --> 00:02:10.900
who are looking at those questions,

00:02:10.900 --> 00:02:12.680
who are going to be surfacing them up

00:02:12.680 --> 00:02:15.140
on my tablet right over here.

00:02:15.140 --> 00:02:18.200
And so I'll know who to
answer questions for.

00:02:18.200 --> 00:02:20.400
You know, just a couple
of updates on our side,

00:02:20.400 --> 00:02:23.570
we are seeing, and I guess
this isn't super-surprising,

00:02:23.570 --> 00:02:27.170
but unusual traffic on Khan Academy.

00:02:27.170 --> 00:02:30.574
What I heard from our
team is Monday we saw 50%

00:02:30.574 --> 00:02:35.000
larger than expected usage
and then yesterday 80%,

00:02:35.000 --> 00:02:39.310
and we're seeing kind of 10X the number

00:02:39.310 --> 00:02:41.350
of teacher and student
and parent registrations.

00:02:41.350 --> 00:02:44.420
So that's great, that it
sounds like a lot of people

00:02:44.420 --> 00:02:46.450
are realizing that this is a
resource that they can turn to.

00:02:46.450 --> 00:02:48.350
It's free, it's accessible.

00:02:48.350 --> 00:02:51.220
And hopefully, as we
go through this crisis,

00:02:51.220 --> 00:02:53.470
folks become more
comfortable with using it.

00:02:53.470 --> 00:02:55.310
And I think there's some
ideas we can talk about

00:02:55.310 --> 00:02:56.600
of how to complement it.

00:02:56.600 --> 00:02:59.260
I do want to say thank
you to Bank of America.

00:02:59.260 --> 00:03:00.720
For those of you who
don't know, Khan Academy,

00:03:00.720 --> 00:03:01.760
we are not-for-profit.

00:03:01.760 --> 00:03:03.280
That means no one owns Khan Academy.

00:03:03.280 --> 00:03:04.610
We are not a company.

00:03:04.610 --> 00:03:05.750
I don't own Khan Academy.

00:03:05.750 --> 00:03:08.070
You own as much of Khan Academy as I do.

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:10.120
And we're funded through philanthropy.

00:03:10.120 --> 00:03:13.573
And we love that we're able
to serve everyone right now

00:03:13.573 --> 00:03:16.180
as we have this crisis.

00:03:16.180 --> 00:03:19.320
But our server costs are
going through the roof.

00:03:19.320 --> 00:03:20.210
We're having demand,

00:03:20.210 --> 00:03:22.840
more folks to be able to
do things like webinars.

00:03:22.840 --> 00:03:24.210
We had a very large parent webinar

00:03:24.210 --> 00:03:26.613
that had a thousand
parents on it last night.

00:03:26.613 --> 00:03:29.310
And so it's really valuable

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:30.540
that Bank of America stepped up.

00:03:30.540 --> 00:03:31.760
You could do a Google search

00:03:31.760 --> 00:03:32.960
and read about that press release.

00:03:32.960 --> 00:03:34.787
But they stepped up to
really support this,

00:03:34.787 --> 00:03:37.470
you can almost call it a relief effort,

00:03:37.470 --> 00:03:39.330
that we're trying to up
our game at Khan Academy

00:03:39.330 --> 00:03:41.187
to make sure everyone feels
connected and supported.

00:03:41.187 --> 00:03:44.000
And expect to see more from us

00:03:44.000 --> 00:03:46.060
and our partners like Bank of America.

00:03:46.060 --> 00:03:48.600
So thank you to Bank of America for that.

00:03:48.600 --> 00:03:51.130
But let me get to,

00:03:51.130 --> 00:03:54.080
let me get to some of the questions here.

00:03:54.080 --> 00:03:57.357
So the first question is,

00:03:57.357 --> 00:04:01.797
"How do I figure out what my
child should be learning?"

00:04:01.797 --> 00:04:04.300
So there's a couple of
ways to answer that.

00:04:04.300 --> 00:04:05.890
Obviously, as a parent,

00:04:05.890 --> 00:04:08.250
you might know what
grade your student's in.

00:04:08.250 --> 00:04:10.192
And you might say, "Okay, my
student's a fifth grader."

00:04:10.192 --> 00:04:12.710
That might be an
indication that fifth grade

00:04:12.710 --> 00:04:15.560
on Khan Academy might be a
really good place to start.

00:04:15.560 --> 00:04:18.820
I will put a little asterisk
there, little bit of a caveat.

00:04:18.820 --> 00:04:21.690
What we found, even when working
with traditional schools,

00:04:21.690 --> 00:04:23.570
is the reason why a lot
of kids have trouble,

00:04:23.570 --> 00:04:24.770
and this is especially true in math,

00:04:24.770 --> 00:04:27.180
but it's also true in things
like reading and writing,

00:04:27.180 --> 00:04:29.080
but especially in math,
they might have trouble

00:04:29.080 --> 00:04:30.640
in fifth, sixth, seventh grade math.

00:04:30.640 --> 00:04:32.150
It's not because they're not bright.

00:04:32.150 --> 00:04:34.930
It's not because the
subject matter is difficult.

00:04:34.930 --> 00:04:36.240
It's because as they went through

00:04:36.240 --> 00:04:39.191
their academic foundations,
they had some gaps.

00:04:39.191 --> 00:04:41.190
They might have been in third grade

00:04:41.190 --> 00:04:42.980
when they were learning
multiplication tables.

00:04:42.980 --> 00:04:46.140
They just didn't learn their
seven times tables well.

00:04:46.140 --> 00:04:47.610
And they got a C on a test,

00:04:47.610 --> 00:04:49.250
and then they just moved
on to the next thing.

00:04:49.250 --> 00:04:51.700
And so when they see, have to
multiply something by seven

00:04:51.700 --> 00:04:53.300
in sixth grade, they just have to spend

00:04:53.300 --> 00:04:54.830
a little bit too much cognitive load.

00:04:54.830 --> 00:04:56.967
Or their decimals in
fifth grade are a gap.

00:04:56.967 --> 00:04:59.980
And so when they see a decimal
in seventh grade, it's a gap.

00:04:59.980 --> 00:05:02.370
So one thing we've seen
teachers do to great effect,

00:05:02.370 --> 00:05:04.040
there's an amazing
teacher, Tim Vandenberg,

00:05:04.040 --> 00:05:05.412
who I was talking to two days ago.

00:05:05.412 --> 00:05:07.640
He was telling me how,
with his sixth graders,

00:05:07.640 --> 00:05:10.310
he starts them all on
kindergarten on Khan Academy.

00:05:10.310 --> 00:05:11.770
And if you know the material,

00:05:11.770 --> 00:05:13.770
the students can go through
each course quite quickly.

00:05:13.770 --> 00:05:16.210
We have things like Course
Challenges, Unit Tests,

00:05:16.210 --> 00:05:19.460
Mastery Challenges that can
accelerate students through it.

00:05:19.460 --> 00:05:22.160
That helps them build some
momentum, build some confidence,

00:05:22.160 --> 00:05:23.661
get familiarity with the platform,

00:05:23.661 --> 00:05:26.350
and then it ensures that
they don't have any gaps.

00:05:26.350 --> 00:05:28.790
So he has his students do early learning

00:05:28.790 --> 00:05:31.610
and then do the arithmetic
courses on Khan Academy

00:05:31.610 --> 00:05:32.660
and the third grade courses.

00:05:32.660 --> 00:05:35.680
He'd use those as the foundational
ones and simultaneously

00:05:35.680 --> 00:05:38.110
work on the grade-level
course on Khan Academy.

00:05:38.110 --> 00:05:40.180
If your student has, for the most part,

00:05:40.180 --> 00:05:42.450
been proficient in math,
then I would go straight

00:05:42.450 --> 00:05:44.251
to the grade-level course on Khan Academy.

00:05:44.251 --> 00:05:47.370
If, since we're already
well into the year,

00:05:47.370 --> 00:05:49.150
I would have your child,

00:05:49.150 --> 00:05:51.030
and if you're a student, I would have you

00:05:51.030 --> 00:05:52.420
take the Course Challenge,

00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:54.670
and the Course Challenge
will sample every unit.

00:05:54.670 --> 00:05:56.170
And if you take the Course Challenge

00:05:56.170 --> 00:05:57.600
and you get an 80 or 90% on it,

00:05:57.600 --> 00:05:59.320
keep taking the Course Challenge

00:05:59.320 --> 00:06:01.130
and try to get higher and higher scores

00:06:01.130 --> 00:06:02.240
until you have mastery in the course.

00:06:02.240 --> 00:06:03.940
But if you see there's some gaps

00:06:03.940 --> 00:06:05.910
that you're consistently
not getting right,

00:06:05.910 --> 00:06:06.860
then those skills,

00:06:06.860 --> 00:06:08.990
you can look at those
units that they come from,

00:06:08.990 --> 00:06:11.260
and then you can ensure that
you get mastery in those units.

00:06:11.260 --> 00:06:12.660
And so that's a way that we're,

00:06:12.660 --> 00:06:15.040
you know, we're 3/4 or
80% through the year,

00:06:15.040 --> 00:06:17.270
that you can quickly know what you know

00:06:17.270 --> 00:06:18.530
and what you don't know and where you need

00:06:18.530 --> 00:06:19.790
to actually fill in your gaps.

00:06:19.790 --> 00:06:22.820
So that's my personal view of
the quickest way to figure out

00:06:22.820 --> 00:06:26.370
where might be the right zone for you.

00:06:26.370 --> 00:06:28.040
And the same thing is true
at the high school level.

00:06:28.040 --> 00:06:28.989
You know, even high school students,

00:06:28.989 --> 00:06:31.170
doesn't hurt starting a little bit behind.

00:06:31.170 --> 00:06:32.430
You have more time now,

00:06:32.430 --> 00:06:33.960
and make sure you fill in those gaps.

00:06:33.960 --> 00:06:36.280
And things like our SAT Practice

00:06:36.280 --> 00:06:38.530
that we do with the College
Board, that adapts to you.

00:06:38.530 --> 00:06:41.600
So if you start getting questions
of a certain skill wrong,

00:06:41.600 --> 00:06:42.990
it'll give you slightly easier ones

00:06:42.990 --> 00:06:44.680
or then slightly harder
ones if you get 'em right.

00:06:44.680 --> 00:06:47.379
So that's my best advice there.

00:06:47.379 --> 00:06:49.170
You know, some people have been asking

00:06:49.170 --> 00:06:50.650
for just general tips.

00:06:50.650 --> 00:06:53.480
And I was on some of
the morning shows today,

00:06:53.480 --> 00:06:58.100
and what I say is make sure
you have some structure today.

00:06:58.100 --> 00:07:00.809
As we get more days into
the social distancing,

00:07:00.809 --> 00:07:03.570
it's very tempting to stay in your pajamas

00:07:03.570 --> 00:07:06.310
and kinda try to work from bed

00:07:06.310 --> 00:07:07.760
with the TV running and all that.

00:07:07.760 --> 00:07:08.810
That's not going to be good,

00:07:08.810 --> 00:07:10.910
and it's not good even for
your own mental health.

00:07:10.910 --> 00:07:12.630
It's good to have a pattern.

00:07:12.630 --> 00:07:13.470
Wake up in the morning,

00:07:13.470 --> 00:07:15.110
just like you would have done for school.

00:07:15.110 --> 00:07:16.230
Have your breakfast.

00:07:16.230 --> 00:07:17.063
Get ready.

00:07:17.063 --> 00:07:18.660
I even heard about some people pretending

00:07:18.660 --> 00:07:20.850
like they're on a bus for some period.

00:07:20.850 --> 00:07:22.970
I guess that's maybe a form of meditation.

00:07:22.970 --> 00:07:24.790
And then break into the schedule.

00:07:24.790 --> 00:07:26.330
And we, at Khan Academy,

00:07:26.330 --> 00:07:29.580
we've released some schedules
for school closures,

00:07:29.580 --> 00:07:31.670
and it's for different schedules
for different age groups

00:07:31.670 --> 00:07:33.530
that have different amounts breaks

00:07:33.530 --> 00:07:34.679
and things like that in them.

00:07:34.679 --> 00:07:36.050
But use those.

00:07:36.050 --> 00:07:37.810
You could use those just as they are,

00:07:37.810 --> 00:07:40.900
or you can modify them to meet your needs.

00:07:40.900 --> 00:07:42.330
But if you have that schedule,

00:07:42.330 --> 00:07:43.610
then that also gives you a clearer sense,

00:07:43.610 --> 00:07:45.340
as, hey, if I get all
this done by one o'clock

00:07:45.340 --> 00:07:47.790
in the afternoon or two
o'clock, I can then relax,

00:07:47.790 --> 00:07:49.330
I can then go do other things,

00:07:49.330 --> 00:07:52.380
pursue my passions, work on other hobbies.

00:07:52.380 --> 00:07:53.919
Ideally, you have a place in the house

00:07:53.919 --> 00:07:56.650
that is nice and clean, free of clutter,

00:07:56.650 --> 00:07:58.700
where you just associate that with work.

00:07:58.700 --> 00:08:01.610
And that spatial association, I think,

00:08:01.610 --> 00:08:04.330
will help your productivity.

00:08:04.330 --> 00:08:09.330
All right, so we have
Sayed Mohel Rosales asked

00:08:11.747 --> 00:08:14.210
"Will Khan Academy always be free?"

00:08:14.210 --> 00:08:15.330
Simple answer is yes.

00:08:15.330 --> 00:08:17.880
You know our mission statement
is free world-class education

00:08:17.880 --> 00:08:19.550
for anyone, anywhere.

00:08:19.550 --> 00:08:22.340
The whole idea about
being a not-for-profit,

00:08:22.340 --> 00:08:23.950
there's nothing wrong
with for-profit companies.

00:08:23.950 --> 00:08:25.610
Most of my career I'd worked at some form

00:08:25.610 --> 00:08:27.670
of a for-profit or an other.

00:08:27.670 --> 00:08:28.503
But a for-profit company
at the end of the day,

00:08:28.503 --> 00:08:31.200
their bottom line literally, is,

00:08:31.200 --> 00:08:33.860
you know maximizing
shareholder value or income,

00:08:33.860 --> 00:08:34.950
or things like that.

00:08:34.950 --> 00:08:36.876
Nothing wrong with that,
that's how capitalism works.

00:08:36.876 --> 00:08:39.940
But a not-for-profit, its
bottom line is its mission,

00:08:39.940 --> 00:08:40.970
and our mission is free,

00:08:40.970 --> 00:08:43.757
world-class education
for anyone, anywhere.

00:08:43.757 --> 00:08:45.030
And the reason why we set it up

00:08:45.030 --> 00:08:46.950
as a not-for-profit you know twelve

00:08:46.950 --> 00:08:48.270
years ago I could have never foreseen

00:08:48.270 --> 00:08:51.190
this scenario is because there's certain

00:08:51.190 --> 00:08:53.590
parts of our market economy where

00:08:53.590 --> 00:08:55.840
market forces alone
might not work properly,

00:08:55.840 --> 00:08:57.470
or if they did work properly, it might not

00:08:57.470 --> 00:08:59.060
lead to the types of outcomes we want.

00:08:59.060 --> 00:09:01.130
And you know, I frankly, I
think education and healthcare

00:09:01.130 --> 00:09:02.890
are the two areas where you,

00:09:02.890 --> 00:09:04.500
you don't want someone's access to it

00:09:04.500 --> 00:09:06.670
to be gated by their ability to pay,

00:09:06.670 --> 00:09:08.690
or you know, what their
parents do for a living,

00:09:08.690 --> 00:09:10.620
or that you know, things like that.

00:09:10.620 --> 00:09:12.590
And so that's why Khan Academy is free,

00:09:12.590 --> 00:09:14.160
and will always be free.

00:09:14.160 --> 00:09:15.690
And it's not, and it's not-for-profit.

00:09:15.690 --> 00:09:19.200
It's primarily funded through
philanthropic donations,

00:09:19.200 --> 00:09:21.867
So Tada Bevell says,
"Frustrating trying to help

00:09:21.867 --> 00:09:24.387
"my college engineering son
with his Internet issues."

00:09:24.387 --> 00:09:25.807
"He also needs tutoring in calculus two,

00:09:25.807 --> 00:09:27.900
"and physics, any help?"

00:09:27.900 --> 00:09:29.950
So depends what the Internet issues are.

00:09:29.950 --> 00:09:33.011
I don't have good advice for
if there's connectivity issues.

00:09:33.011 --> 00:09:35.150
Maybe the local carrier,

00:09:35.150 --> 00:09:37.040
whoever might might be able
to help with some of that.

00:09:37.040 --> 00:09:39.080
But if he's taking
calculus two and physics,

00:09:39.080 --> 00:09:40.454
we can help there.

00:09:40.454 --> 00:09:43.100
Khan Academy has a full,

00:09:43.100 --> 00:09:45.670
depending on which college you go to,

00:09:45.670 --> 00:09:47.235
first two semesters or three semesters

00:09:47.235 --> 00:09:50.020
of college calculus, not just videos.

00:09:50.020 --> 00:09:52.650
We have exercises, you get
as much practice as you need.

00:09:52.650 --> 00:09:54.410
So I really encourage
your son to get on that.

00:09:54.410 --> 00:09:56.970
Make sure he, same advice I
have for the younger students,

00:09:56.970 --> 00:09:58.680
take the course challenge.

00:09:58.680 --> 00:10:00.530
Understand what he knows and doesn't know,

00:10:00.530 --> 00:10:04.730
then he can focus on the units
that he he has some gaps in,

00:10:04.730 --> 00:10:07.260
and then if he's able to
get to, say 90% mastery

00:10:07.260 --> 00:10:10.670
on our, on our college
calculus, or our Calculus BC,

00:10:10.670 --> 00:10:13.600
which is AP, but still a
first-year college course,

00:10:13.600 --> 00:10:14.550
I think he'll do great.

00:10:14.550 --> 00:10:16.830
We have the same thing
in first year physics.

00:10:16.830 --> 00:10:18.000
He could use our AP physics,

00:10:18.000 --> 00:10:19.950
which is really college physics, that I,

00:10:19.950 --> 00:10:22.180
hopefully will, will help him.

00:10:22.180 --> 00:10:26.217
So let's see Alisharova Kilola asks,

00:10:26.217 --> 00:10:28.957
"How do I motivate my
little brother to study

00:10:28.957 --> 00:10:30.577
"during the quarantine?"

00:10:30.577 --> 00:10:33.300
"He really doesn't want to do anything."

00:10:33.300 --> 00:10:36.421
And Alisharova, I empathize with you.

00:10:36.421 --> 00:10:38.501
I know I have three kids at home.

00:10:38.501 --> 00:10:41.380
And the older two, I have to
give them a lot of credit.

00:10:41.380 --> 00:10:45.330
11 and and eight years old,
they are really productive.

00:10:45.330 --> 00:10:47.380
I think even more productive than me.

00:10:47.380 --> 00:10:49.130
Their school's doing a,
you know they're getting

00:10:49.130 --> 00:10:50.899
a list of stuff to do every day.

00:10:50.899 --> 00:10:53.940
They're, they're connecting on
Hangouts with their teachers

00:10:53.940 --> 00:10:55.120
at appropriate times.

00:10:55.120 --> 00:10:56.530
So they're fine, but my five-year-old,

00:10:56.530 --> 00:10:59.940
I've been having a lot of
difficulty motivating him.

00:10:59.940 --> 00:11:02.160
I think he's just getting
used to the circumstances

00:11:02.160 --> 00:11:03.750
that we're all finding ourselves in.

00:11:03.750 --> 00:11:05.470
We had a good tip from
a homeschooling parent

00:11:05.470 --> 00:11:07.940
a couple of days ago on this livestream,

00:11:07.940 --> 00:11:09.500
where she said you just
have to stick with it.

00:11:09.500 --> 00:11:10.440
At first it's hard,

00:11:10.440 --> 00:11:12.300
everyone's getting used
to the same patterns,

00:11:12.300 --> 00:11:13.610
but it's just about sticking with it,

00:11:13.610 --> 00:11:14.780
maintaining a pattern.

00:11:14.780 --> 00:11:18.740
Having consistency, you
know, for very young kids.

00:11:18.740 --> 00:11:20.560
I hope none of y'all are,
well I hope you are listening,

00:11:20.560 --> 00:11:23.540
but my technique with my
five-year-old sometimes the direct

00:11:23.540 --> 00:11:25.610
course of action is not
the ones work on this.

00:11:25.610 --> 00:11:27.267
He will resist but if you say,

00:11:27.267 --> 00:11:29.137
"Hey, that was really
amazing how many points

00:11:29.137 --> 00:11:29.990
"you got yesterday!"

00:11:29.990 --> 00:11:32.100
Then you know, kind of
strokes his ego a little bit,

00:11:32.100 --> 00:11:35.030
he gets a little excited about that.

00:11:35.030 --> 00:11:36.887
Or if you say, "Hey
tell me about that song

00:11:36.887 --> 00:11:37.720
"you really like."

00:11:37.720 --> 00:11:39.700
And then while, while he
tells you about the song,

00:11:39.700 --> 00:11:41.492
I'd kind of open up the
work he needs to do,

00:11:41.492 --> 00:11:44.000
handwriting or whatever else.

00:11:44.000 --> 00:11:46.090
So, that's one possibility.

00:11:46.090 --> 00:11:47.740
But I think, you know,
this is one of those times,

00:11:47.740 --> 00:11:48.821
don't beat up yourself.

00:11:48.821 --> 00:11:50.370
It's really impressive, as a sibling,

00:11:50.370 --> 00:11:51.802
you're trying to help your sibling.

00:11:51.802 --> 00:11:54.530
But you know, don't put too
much pressure on yourself.

00:11:54.530 --> 00:11:56.910
Do what you can, keep some consistency.

00:11:56.910 --> 00:11:59.660
And what the homeschooling
parents are telling us

00:11:59.660 --> 00:12:01.987
is that, "Oh it's still the first week

00:12:01.987 --> 00:12:04.577
"for most of us, and
so you know it's okay."

00:12:04.577 --> 00:12:07.969
"We can we can give all
of ourselves a break,

00:12:07.969 --> 00:12:08.870
"and you know."

00:12:08.870 --> 00:12:10.100
And this is the other thing

00:12:10.100 --> 00:12:12.120
we're gonna probably form some habits

00:12:12.120 --> 00:12:14.210
over the next month or two as the

00:12:14.210 --> 00:12:16.000
schools are closed and it sounds like, it

00:12:16.000 --> 00:12:17.420
in many places they will be closed

00:12:17.420 --> 00:12:18.280
to the end of the school year.

00:12:18.280 --> 00:12:20.100
We're hearing that in California.

00:12:20.100 --> 00:12:22.960
But know we have the whole
summer to continue learning,

00:12:22.960 --> 00:12:25.120
to, you know, catch up if
we need to fill in gaps.

00:12:25.120 --> 00:12:26.030
And then continue learning.

00:12:26.030 --> 00:12:28.790
So as long as people are
able to ramp into the summer

00:12:28.790 --> 00:12:29.900
and keep learning during the summer,

00:12:29.900 --> 00:12:31.550
I'm not worried that
they're going to be able

00:12:31.550 --> 00:12:34.163
to do just fine when schools reopen.

00:12:35.240 --> 00:12:39.097
All right, so someone says
"How will Khan Academy

00:12:39.097 --> 00:12:41.907
"get me into places like
Stanford University?"

00:12:41.907 --> 00:12:43.437
"I understand", oh "I understand.

00:12:43.437 --> 00:12:44.757
"a guy who was locked up for 15 years

00:12:44.757 --> 00:12:46.750
"used your resources
and got into Stanford."

00:12:46.750 --> 00:12:50.970
Okay, so Adam, Adam Bukhari
asked that question.

00:12:50.970 --> 00:12:54.520
So I'll first give my disclaimer,
where you go to college,

00:12:54.520 --> 00:12:57.200
just does not define you as a human being.

00:12:57.200 --> 00:13:00.190
I think too many times in our society,

00:13:00.190 --> 00:13:02.840
and a lot of the signals
you get in high school,

00:13:02.840 --> 00:13:04.150
and, and when you're young,

00:13:04.150 --> 00:13:06.250
from your friends are
like, you're defined.

00:13:06.250 --> 00:13:07.760
But you know you try it you're trying to

00:13:07.760 --> 00:13:08.900
find your identity and you might be

00:13:08.900 --> 00:13:11.010
defined by your test scores,
or where you go to college,

00:13:11.010 --> 00:13:13.330
et cetera, et cetera, and you,

00:13:13.330 --> 00:13:15.140
your life is far more than that.

00:13:15.140 --> 00:13:17.160
Than these test scores, or
where you go to college.

00:13:17.160 --> 00:13:19.807
Now, for those of you
who are, you know, you,

00:13:19.807 --> 00:13:21.580
there's nothing wrong with wanting to go

00:13:21.580 --> 00:13:23.440
to a highly selective University.

00:13:23.440 --> 00:13:25.750
I would just say just
don't be attached to it,

00:13:25.750 --> 00:13:28.070
as Buddha says the course of all the the

00:13:28.070 --> 00:13:29.700
source of all suffering is it you should

00:13:29.700 --> 00:13:31.470
always try your best and it's great to

00:13:31.470 --> 00:13:34.340
be able to find a college
that will challenge you,

00:13:34.340 --> 00:13:36.080
that will put you around other people

00:13:36.080 --> 00:13:38.210
that will make you make you better.

00:13:38.210 --> 00:13:40.010
My best advice, and I tell this,

00:13:40.010 --> 00:13:42.020
you know we have a Lab School downstairs,

00:13:42.020 --> 00:13:43.920
and I tell this to the
students about your age

00:13:43.920 --> 00:13:46.760
is make sure you really master material.

00:13:46.760 --> 00:13:49.905
Don't try to, you know, be, do everything.

00:13:49.905 --> 00:13:51.940
Master the core for sure.

00:13:51.940 --> 00:13:54.118
You know the the
mathematics, the sciences,

00:13:54.118 --> 00:13:56.850
the writing, the humanities.

00:13:56.850 --> 00:13:59.490
But make sure you have time
and space for your passions.

00:13:59.490 --> 00:14:00.760
You know, you see a lot of students

00:14:00.760 --> 00:14:03.380
who, they think they have to do everything

00:14:03.380 --> 00:14:05.280
in order to impress other people.

00:14:05.280 --> 00:14:07.147
And in some ways the
other people will say,

00:14:07.147 --> 00:14:09.017
"That person is just trying to
do everything to impress me."

00:14:09.017 --> 00:14:10.410
"Who are they really?"

00:14:10.410 --> 00:14:12.450
And we, I've talked to a
lot of admissions officers.

00:14:12.450 --> 00:14:15.130
We have some on our advisory
board here at Khan Academy,

00:14:15.130 --> 00:14:17.760
and they all tell us we just
want to see authenticity.

00:14:17.760 --> 00:14:19.160
For sure, they need to see kids

00:14:19.160 --> 00:14:21.080
who they feel confident,
will be able to handle

00:14:21.080 --> 00:14:23.190
the academic rigor of their campus.

00:14:23.190 --> 00:14:24.570
But they just want to see authenticity.

00:14:24.570 --> 00:14:25.970
They want to see curiosity.

00:14:25.970 --> 00:14:27.940
They want to see creativity.

00:14:27.940 --> 00:14:30.570
And so I think, find your passions.

00:14:30.570 --> 00:14:32.340
You have, you know glass half-full,

00:14:32.340 --> 00:14:34.750
the school closures, you might
have a little more time now.

00:14:34.750 --> 00:14:36.751
This is a great time to
explore your passions,

00:14:36.751 --> 00:14:39.340
find solutions for people
in your neighborhood.

00:14:39.340 --> 00:14:41.260
There's all sorts of people
who need help right now.

00:14:41.260 --> 00:14:43.660
Do it authentically, don't do
it just to get into college.

00:14:43.660 --> 00:14:44.937
Say, "Hey, there might be senior citizens

00:14:44.937 --> 00:14:45.937
"who can't go to the grocery store."

00:14:45.937 --> 00:14:47.297
"Maybe I can drop off the groceries

00:14:47.297 --> 00:14:48.527
"out there at their porch."

00:14:48.527 --> 00:14:50.137
"Maybe I can turn into a
thing me and my friends

00:14:50.137 --> 00:14:51.730
"can volunteer to do that"

00:14:51.730 --> 00:14:54.730
If you do it authentically,
then when it's time

00:14:54.730 --> 00:14:57.530
to apply to college, and if
you're academically prepared,

00:14:57.530 --> 00:14:58.850
you've given it your best shot.

00:14:58.850 --> 00:15:00.230
And so that's that's my best advice.

00:15:00.230 --> 00:15:04.030
You know the example you
gave, of Jason who was,

00:15:04.030 --> 00:15:05.200
for those of you who don't know the story,

00:15:05.200 --> 00:15:07.296
this was a young man,
when he was in his teens,

00:15:07.296 --> 00:15:09.220
he got arrested for selling marijuana.

00:15:09.220 --> 00:15:11.323
He got thirty years in prison in Illinois.

00:15:12.480 --> 00:15:15.180
While in prison, his
mother gave him transcripts

00:15:15.180 --> 00:15:16.210
of Khan Academy videos

00:15:16.210 --> 00:15:17.043
so he could start to learn.

00:15:17.043 --> 00:15:18.770
They had no access to the Internet.

00:15:18.770 --> 00:15:22.210
He had his sentence shortened to 15 years.

00:15:22.210 --> 00:15:24.190
So he gets out in his early 30s,

00:15:24.190 --> 00:15:26.460
and then he gets on Khan Academy for real

00:15:26.460 --> 00:15:29.340
at the local library, uses
our official SAT practice,

00:15:29.340 --> 00:15:31.780
and he's able to kind of catch up,

00:15:31.780 --> 00:15:34.250
and he initially said
his local music college.

00:15:34.250 --> 00:15:36.840
But he decides that he
wants to apply to Stanford.

00:15:36.840 --> 00:15:38.400
And he was able to
transfer, and I met him.

00:15:38.400 --> 00:15:40.180
And that's how I even know the story.

00:15:40.180 --> 00:15:42.160
So there's some, I don't
say that is the way

00:15:42.160 --> 00:15:43.790
that you know, you should go to Stanford.

00:15:43.790 --> 00:15:44.690
But I'm saying that as a way

00:15:44.690 --> 00:15:47.470
that I think anyone,
if you apply yourself,

00:15:47.470 --> 00:15:48.850
you put your best foot forward,

00:15:48.850 --> 00:15:50.030
and you're authentic about it.

00:15:50.030 --> 00:15:51.550
You know I would say
what's really powerful

00:15:51.550 --> 00:15:53.750
about Jason's story is
how authentic he is,

00:15:53.750 --> 00:15:55.464
and how much he owns his own history.

00:15:55.464 --> 00:15:57.310
And I think that's really
powerful, and people would

00:15:57.310 --> 00:15:58.880
be impressed by that.

00:15:58.880 --> 00:16:02.767
Let's see Abed Sheik says, "Do
you have any science classes

00:16:02.767 --> 00:16:04.770
"for middle school on Khan Academy?"

00:16:04.770 --> 00:16:06.960
So obviously that is actually

00:16:06.960 --> 00:16:08.730
a gap that we have, officially.

00:16:08.730 --> 00:16:11.600
We don't have middle
school science, per se.

00:16:11.600 --> 00:16:13.630
With that said, what I will tell you

00:16:13.630 --> 00:16:16.530
is I think the high school
biology is completely doable

00:16:16.530 --> 00:16:18.970
by a middle school student.

00:16:18.970 --> 00:16:21.030
The math prerequisites you
would have already seen

00:16:21.030 --> 00:16:23.190
in middle school, things
like negative numbers,

00:16:23.190 --> 00:16:25.920
and decimals, and exponents,
and things like that.

00:16:25.920 --> 00:16:27.850
And so, and especially
because high school biology,

00:16:27.850 --> 00:16:28.950
super interesting right now,

00:16:28.950 --> 00:16:32.640
because you'll learn about
viruses, and DNA and RNA.

00:16:32.640 --> 00:16:35.110
And you know that's very
relevant to the world we're at,

00:16:35.110 --> 00:16:37.010
so I would recommend high school students

00:16:37.010 --> 00:16:38.335
to work on the high school,

00:16:38.335 --> 00:16:40.930
sorry, middle school students
to work on the high school

00:16:40.930 --> 00:16:42.600
biology on Khan Academy.

00:16:42.600 --> 00:16:43.700
I think that could keep you busy,

00:16:43.700 --> 00:16:44.860
and you'll learn a lot and that's just

00:16:44.860 --> 00:16:46.993
a useful, useful skill for life.

00:16:47.900 --> 00:16:52.900
All right, so, so Lethal
Hipster, interesting name,

00:16:53.710 --> 00:16:56.977
says, "Hi, Khan, how long
have you been teaching?"

00:16:58.320 --> 00:17:00.480
Well, depends how you define teaching.

00:17:00.480 --> 00:17:03.550
You know I, I've taught in various

00:17:03.550 --> 00:17:06.610
formal and informal settings
over over many years.

00:17:06.610 --> 00:17:08.830
If I were to go really
far back, the first time

00:17:08.830 --> 00:17:11.560
that I realized that I enjoyed tutoring

00:17:11.560 --> 00:17:13.260
or teaching was in high school.

00:17:13.260 --> 00:17:14.500
It might not surprise people,

00:17:14.500 --> 00:17:16.320
but I was the president of the math club.

00:17:16.320 --> 00:17:18.950
I assure you, it was cooler
than you might imagine,

00:17:18.950 --> 00:17:20.360
and as part of that we had,

00:17:20.360 --> 00:17:21.600
we used to tutor a lot of our,

00:17:21.600 --> 00:17:24.470
we had free tutoring for
everyone at our school.

00:17:24.470 --> 00:17:27.150
And I saw over and over
again that students,

00:17:27.150 --> 00:17:29.830
my friends who thought
they weren't good at math,

00:17:29.830 --> 00:17:31.890
if they just had a
little bit of attention,

00:17:31.890 --> 00:17:33.890
a little bit of being able
to fill in their gaps,

00:17:33.890 --> 00:17:35.197
then all of a sudden were like,

00:17:35.197 --> 00:17:36.543
"Wait, this isn't so bad."

00:17:36.543 --> 00:17:39.010
And, and then they were,
they were off to the races.

00:17:39.010 --> 00:17:41.210
And so that's what first
stuck in my head of

00:17:42.250 --> 00:17:43.560
the reason why people are held back

00:17:43.560 --> 00:17:45.150
isn't because of their ability,

00:17:45.150 --> 00:17:46.300
it's because of these gaps.

00:17:46.300 --> 00:17:47.500
And they just need a little bit of

00:17:47.500 --> 00:17:50.570
personalization, so to
speak, to fill in those gaps.

00:17:50.570 --> 00:17:55.570
Then in college I, I did some,
actually I taught at the,

00:17:56.030 --> 00:17:59.210
there's a local school district
in Brookline, Massachusetts

00:17:59.210 --> 00:18:01.080
and they had lost funding
for some of their classes.

00:18:01.080 --> 00:18:02.780
And so they were looking
for volunteer teachers.

00:18:02.780 --> 00:18:05.930
So when I was in college
it was actually myself,

00:18:05.930 --> 00:18:08.050
and my roommate, who's
named Shantanu Sinha,

00:18:08.050 --> 00:18:10.930
and he was actually the first
president of Khan Academy.

00:18:10.930 --> 00:18:15.030
We used to go volunteer teach
at Brookline Devotion School.

00:18:15.030 --> 00:18:17.270
This was in Brookline, Massachusetts

00:18:17.270 --> 00:18:19.150
in middle school students.

00:18:19.150 --> 00:18:21.660
And then later in college
I actually was doing

00:18:21.660 --> 00:18:24.080
some test flight, test
prep stuff for for folks

00:18:24.080 --> 00:18:24.913
like Princeton Review.

00:18:24.913 --> 00:18:26.590
And then my career took
a completely different,

00:18:26.590 --> 00:18:28.260
I went into tech, et cetera, et cetera.

00:18:28.260 --> 00:18:30.400
But I was always fascinated
by this whole teaching side.

00:18:30.400 --> 00:18:33.710
And so when my cousins,
in 2004, needed help,

00:18:33.710 --> 00:18:34.687
I was like, "Hey can I tutor you?"

00:18:34.687 --> 00:18:36.527
"'Cause I just love,
it's just fun to be able

00:18:36.527 --> 00:18:37.360
"to connect with people."

00:18:37.360 --> 00:18:38.890
"I love the subject matter."

00:18:38.890 --> 00:18:40.560
And then that's kind of how I got started

00:18:40.560 --> 00:18:42.370
on this this Khan Academy adventure.

00:18:42.370 --> 00:18:44.930
That was about 16 years ago.

00:18:44.930 --> 00:18:46.890
Yesterday someone commented
about how old I look,

00:18:46.890 --> 00:18:48.473
and so that's why.

00:18:48.473 --> 00:18:50.556
(laughs)

00:18:52.000 --> 00:18:54.557
Someone is asking, Andy says

00:18:54.557 --> 00:18:56.007
"Hey Sal, it's 8:17 AM here,

00:18:56.007 --> 00:18:58.297
"and I'm about to live with

00:18:58.297 --> 00:18:59.647
"schedules that you planned."

00:18:59.647 --> 00:19:00.480
"Thank you so much!"

00:19:00.480 --> 00:19:01.490
Andy, great news!

00:19:01.490 --> 00:19:02.480
And yeah, my apologies.

00:19:02.480 --> 00:19:03.700
I know these livestreams conflict

00:19:03.700 --> 00:19:05.050
with the same schedules that we publish,

00:19:05.050 --> 00:19:05.883
and it's hard.

00:19:05.883 --> 00:19:07.580
'Cause those schedules are
whatever time zone you're on,

00:19:07.580 --> 00:19:09.030
and obviously this live
stream is happening

00:19:09.030 --> 00:19:12.020
at different times, depending
on what time zone you are in.

00:19:12.020 --> 00:19:13.690
But I'm glad you're using the schedules.

00:19:13.690 --> 00:19:16.780
And we are going to look
for more ways to support

00:19:16.780 --> 00:19:18.910
those schedules and we
can get you support.

00:19:18.910 --> 00:19:21.100
See, it says "How can I
learn personal finance

00:19:21.100 --> 00:19:22.167
at Khan Academy?"

00:19:23.080 --> 00:19:24.810
You could literally look at our menus.

00:19:24.810 --> 00:19:26.630
There's a course menu when
you go to the top left.

00:19:26.630 --> 00:19:28.490
And Finance and Personal Finance

00:19:28.490 --> 00:19:30.320
are sections on Khan Academy.

00:19:30.320 --> 00:19:31.750
Those don't have the exercises

00:19:31.750 --> 00:19:33.730
the way that math and other subjects do.

00:19:33.730 --> 00:19:35.170
But there's a lot of videos there,

00:19:35.170 --> 00:19:36.920
and I think if you look at those videos,

00:19:36.920 --> 00:19:38.940
and especially if if I'm
working through a problem,

00:19:38.940 --> 00:19:41.120
you pause and you try
to do it on your own.

00:19:41.120 --> 00:19:43.820
You'll be surprised how
much personal finance

00:19:43.820 --> 00:19:44.653
you might learn.

00:19:44.653 --> 00:19:45.486
And I'm happy to answer actually

00:19:45.486 --> 00:19:48.450
any personal finance
questions if anyone has any.

00:19:48.450 --> 00:19:49.760
