00:19:52.950 --> 00:19:54.530
they're having questions
about the stock market,

00:19:54.530 --> 00:19:56.220
and my, you know my old life I used to be

00:19:56.220 --> 00:19:57.080
an investment analyst.

00:19:57.080 --> 00:20:00.480
So I don't mind answering
questions about that.

00:20:00.480 --> 00:20:03.247
Okay so Christine Priti,

00:20:03.247 --> 00:20:05.007
"Will you be adding
more courses, et cetera

00:20:05.007 --> 00:20:07.080
"if schools stay closed
for a longer time?"

00:20:07.080 --> 00:20:08.720
A great question Christine.

00:20:08.720 --> 00:20:12.390
You know we, we're looking
to do anything and everything

00:20:12.390 --> 00:20:14.060
necessary to be able to support folks

00:20:14.060 --> 00:20:16.600
through this school closure situation.

00:20:16.600 --> 00:20:17.433
It looks like they

00:20:17.433 --> 00:20:19.210
will be closed and you know in many

00:20:19.210 --> 00:20:20.470
places through the end of the year.

00:20:20.470 --> 00:20:22.010
We're talking, you know
everything we do is

00:20:22.010 --> 00:20:24.700
philanthropically funded, so
you know we talked earlier

00:20:24.700 --> 00:20:26.410
about the the gap we have
in middle school science.

00:20:26.410 --> 00:20:28.130
I would love nothing more
than to be able to create

00:20:28.130 --> 00:20:30.360
some middle school science
content on Khan Academy.

00:20:30.360 --> 00:20:33.340
We need to find philanthropists.

00:20:33.340 --> 00:20:36.030
Small and large philanthropists,
every donation counts.

00:20:36.030 --> 00:20:38.280
So that we can start
hiring up a team of folks

00:20:38.280 --> 00:20:40.590
who can start helping build that content.

00:20:40.590 --> 00:20:41.598
You know a lot of time I get

00:20:41.598 --> 00:20:43.480
disproportionate credit for Khan Academy

00:20:43.480 --> 00:20:44.690
'cause I make a lot of the videos.

00:20:44.690 --> 00:20:47.300
And you know I'm kind of the mascot,

00:20:47.300 --> 00:20:48.211
for better or for worse.

00:20:48.211 --> 00:20:50.140
I'm not so pleasant of a mascot.

00:20:50.140 --> 00:20:53.470
but there's a lot of
people behind the scenes.

00:20:53.470 --> 00:20:56.770
We have over 200 employees
who have salaries

00:20:56.770 --> 00:20:57.670
that we need to be able to

00:20:57.670 --> 00:20:59.510
pay so that they can do their work.

00:20:59.510 --> 00:21:01.630
Most of these people
have taken salary cuts

00:21:01.630 --> 00:21:04.160
from what they could be doing
in the for-profit sector

00:21:04.160 --> 00:21:06.670
because they're so passionate
about this mission.

00:21:06.670 --> 00:21:08.950
And on the content side
I have a ton of support

00:21:08.950 --> 00:21:11.700
from people, oftentimes
with PhDs in the subject,

00:21:11.700 --> 00:21:15.310
who are curating what the,
what the sequence should be,

00:21:15.310 --> 00:21:16.760
how we cover the standards,

00:21:16.760 --> 00:21:18.700
writing these thousands of items

00:21:18.700 --> 00:21:20.860
that we have to write per course,

00:21:20.860 --> 00:21:22.650
getting it vetted with
outside third parties.

00:21:22.650 --> 00:21:24.360
So there's a lot of work that
goes on behind the scenes

00:21:24.360 --> 00:21:26.391
for those courses, but
I hope that we can add

00:21:26.391 --> 00:21:30.370
more and more courses,
especially if it looks like this,

00:21:30.370 --> 00:21:33.580
this crisis continues for, you know

00:21:33.580 --> 00:21:35.433
beyond what we're thinking right now.

00:21:36.410 --> 00:21:40.740
So let's see, high school
seniors are stressed

00:21:40.740 --> 00:21:44.000
about school closures, no
prom, no graduation ceremony,

00:21:44.000 --> 00:21:45.990
what advice do you have for them?

00:21:45.990 --> 00:21:49.480
So I'm, I'm, I don't know how all of this

00:21:49.480 --> 00:21:50.760
is going to play out,

00:21:50.760 --> 00:21:52.970
and you know there's some part of it

00:21:52.970 --> 00:21:54.160
that's not so great.

00:21:54.160 --> 00:21:58.120
That you're, you, you might
miss out on some of these,

00:21:58.120 --> 00:21:59.240
whatever you want to call them,

00:21:59.240 --> 00:22:01.133
rites of, rites of passage.

00:22:02.320 --> 00:22:03.910
What I would say to you is you

00:22:03.910 --> 00:22:05.407
and your generation,
especially those of you

00:22:05.407 --> 00:22:07.930
who are high school juniors
and seniors right now,

00:22:07.930 --> 00:22:09.470
this is going to define your generation.

00:22:09.470 --> 00:22:13.040
You know when you're my age,
y'all are gonna sit around

00:22:13.040 --> 00:22:14.697
the table and reminisce about 2020.

00:22:14.697 --> 00:22:16.607
"Remember that, remember coronavirus?"

00:22:16.607 --> 00:22:18.970
"Remember what was special
about our generation?"

00:22:18.970 --> 00:22:20.850
And I think that actually
will, in many ways,

00:22:20.850 --> 00:22:24.280
form deeper bonds than you know,

00:22:24.280 --> 00:22:25.750
all of the other things
that you might have done

00:22:25.750 --> 00:22:26.583
in the past.

00:22:26.583 --> 00:22:27.650
And I think there's ways

00:22:27.650 --> 00:22:29.590
that you can still have those experiences.

00:22:29.590 --> 00:22:30.660
Let's say you graduate.

00:22:30.660 --> 00:22:33.183
Let's say we're still in
this, in this quarantine,

00:22:33.183 --> 00:22:36.240
or stay at home policy
and so you're not able

00:22:36.240 --> 00:22:37.770
to get those formal ceremonies.

00:22:37.770 --> 00:22:40.400
Maybe they can happen in
the fall, once ought we

00:22:40.400 --> 00:22:41.233
surface from this.

00:22:41.233 --> 00:22:43.030
Or at a later date, and that,

00:22:43.030 --> 00:22:43.863
that could be fun too.

00:22:43.863 --> 00:22:47.672
You know, I'm supposed to be
involved with a few graduations

00:22:47.672 --> 00:22:50.110
